The chain lightning spell channels electrical energy from the air into a
crackling bolt of lightning that strikes a single target with devastating
force.  Unlike its lesser cousin, lightning bolt, the chain lightning spell
can arc from the initial target to strike additional enemies in the room.

The damage from the initial strike is moderate to heavy, and the victim has
a chance to dodge or resist some of the electrical energy.  A successful
resistance will reduce the damage by half.  Exceptionally well-cast spells
can result in double damage on the initial strike as the lightning arcs with
increased intensity.

After striking the primary target, the lightning can arc to other enemies
in the room.  The number of arcs depends on the caster's intelligence - a
more intelligent caster can sustain more arcs.  Each arc deals reduced
damage: 80 percent on the first arc, 60 percent on the second, and 40
percent on the third.  The lightning can bounce back and forth between two
targets, but requires at least two valid targets in the room to arc at all.
Valid targets are enemies not grouped with the caster.

However, the spell is not without risk.  Should the caster lose control of
the electrical energies, the lightning may arc back and strike the caster
instead, ending the spell immediately without arcing to any enemies.

The spell cannot be cast underwater, as the water grounds out the electrical
energy before it can be properly channeled.  Creatures with immunity to
electricity will be unaffected by this spell.

Chain lightning is available to mages who have mastered the air discipline
and is learned starting at 80 percent learnedness.


